This week, Zambia’s economic narrative is dominated by debt relief optimism and the ambitious Grow Zambia Agenda, even as the government walks a tightrope between fiscal discipline and public needs. Meanwhile, the nation’s sporting arenas are under scrutiny for political infiltration, the health sector is bracing for a strong El Niño, and the cost of living continues to bite commuters. Here is our digest.

## Main Stories

1. **Moody’s projects biggest debt fall in Africa, but interest costs remain a drag** – Moody’s Ratings projects Zambia will record the largest decline in government debt in sub-Saharan Africa, with the country listed among eight with a positive outlook. This follows a staff-level agreement with the IMF on a new $1.472 billion extended credit facility. However, Moody’s cautions that Zambia will still spend about 35% of government revenue on interest payments in 2027 – the highest in the region – so the celebration must be tempered with realism about the fiscal space actually available.

2. **Treasury releases K14.6 billion, but debt and wages eat most of it** – The Ministry of Finance released K14.6 billion in September, with K5.4 billion going to the public service wage bill and another K5.4 billion to debt service and arrears. Only K1.8 billion went to transfers, subsidies and social benefits, including K435.8 million for Social Cash Transfer. Finance Minister Situmbeko Musokotwane insists fiscal discipline will translate into tangible improvements, but the numbers show the room for manoeuvre is thin – and the restoration of excise duty on fuel will hit consumers.

3. **No treasury funds for FRA next season – a risky gamble ahead of El Niño** – Dr Musokotwane has confirmed government will not release treasury funds to the Food Reserve Agency next farming season because it overspent on maize purchases this year. This comes as the FRA is scaling up reserves to over one million tonnes ahead of a projected strong El Niño, with about 1.2 million people at risk of severe food shortages from October to March 2027. The contradiction between building strategic reserves and cutting FRA funding needs urgent clarification.

4. **Government cracks down on political chants at stadia** – The Ministry of Sport has directed FAZ, ZPL and clubs to take firm action against individuals chanting political slogans at football matches, following incidents at the Levy Mwanawasa Stadium and Nkana Stadium. The directive is welcome, but the proof will be in enforcement – clubs have historically been reluctant to police their own supporters, and the ministry has not specified what sanctions will apply.

5. **Health sector plans for El Niño, but funding questions remain** – Health Minister Roma Chilengi says 2027 planning must account for El Niño-induced floods and droughts, with the Zambia National Public Health Institute already linking climate data to malaria and diarrhoea cases. Green Economy Minister Gilbert Siame confirms El Niño is established and expected to intensify. The planning is commendable, but the government has not yet announced dedicated climate-health financing – a gap that will be tested at COP31 in Türkiye.

